notes.mechanics:
I read all five passages in full, header lines included, and checked them sentence by sentence through the spelling, punctuation and typography lens. A few definite grammar and logic errors came up along the way, and I reported those too. None of the files lists any corrections already made, so there were none to check. I could not open the source pages: geographical.co.uk, koolearn and pulitzercenter returned no response or a 403. Search snippets did confirm the k16 source wording: “or primarily, aesthetics” and “defies the grid-based system” are in the original Geographical article, so those are errors in the source itself, not slips introduced in transcription.

k50 has six definite errors, very likely planted test errors: potion, 95%, actual, allocate, decreased, and singular “is crumbling”. I kept two things on purpose. “300 million visitors” in ¶4 against “330 million” in ¶7 is a rounded figure next to an exact one, and the exam text has both. “onetime” is a valid US English adjective.

k14 has nothing to report through my lens. I looked hardest at these and kept them:
- the semicolon before “; and the cost of providing healthcare” (acceptable before a long coordinate clause);
- “entitled “Reimagining health”, supported by…” (British style puts the comma outside the quotes);
- the unhyphenated “near breaking point”, and the hyphens in “hospital-centric” and “long-term conditions”;
- “waitlists” (a standard word);
- “rather than “fix” the NHS” (scare quotes, deliberate);
- the closing question’s commas around “often responsible for services that create health”.

In k13 I kept these:
- “succumbing to temptations of tip creep”: “the temptations” would read more naturally, but the phrase is not definitely wrong;
- “from 10% or less in the 1950s to 15% … to 20% or higher today”;
- “ever-higher” and “no-tipping services” (correctly hyphenated).

In k15 I kept these:
- “an HAP” (read aloud as letters, so “an” is right);
- “an often neglected factor” (hyphenation after “often” is optional);
- “medium- to long-term” (a correct suspended hyphen);
- 36°C against the 37°C coastal heat-wave threshold (consistent with the Indian weather service’s criterion);
- “heat wave”, spelled the same way throughout.

In k16 I kept these:
- the comma before “then proceeded” (two predicates, not a comma splice);
- “de Heere Straat, or Gentlemen’s Street”;
- “criss-cross” and “human-made”;
- British spellings (organised, centre, behaviour).
